---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";



const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Best Fertilizer for Grass", url: "/best-fertilizer-for-grass/" },
    { title: "Best Garden Hoses", url: "/best-garden-hose/" },
    { title: "Best Grass Seed", url: "/best-grass-seed/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "6 Best Robotic Lawn Mowers in 2026: Tested Over a Season";
const description =
  "We ran six robot mowers across a full season — boundary wire, RTK GPS, and vision systems. Cut quality, slope handling, and reliability ranked.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "segway-navimow-x3",
    "name": "Segway Navimow X3 Series",
    "category": "RTK GPS, Wire-Free",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 2800,
    "pros": [
      "RTK GPS setup took under an hour with no boundary wire to install anywhere",
      "Mowed in systematic parallel lines rather than bouncing randomly, producing visible stripes",
      "Handled our measured 30% slope confidently, including turns on the incline",
      "No-go zones around beds can be drawn and redrawn in the app in seconds",
      "Maintained position under partial tree canopy where cheaper GPS units drifted"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Requires a clear-sky mounting location for the reference antenna",
      "Premium pricing compared to boundary-wire alternatives of similar cutting capacity"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best robotic lawn mower for most open lawns. Wire-free setup that genuinely works, with systematic mowing patterns and easy zone editing.",
    "description": "The Navimow X3 is the machine that convinced us wire-free robot mowing has actually arrived. Setup took 50 minutes start to finish: mount the reference antenna on the garage gable, power it, then drive the mower around the lawn perimeter and each flower bed from a phone. No pegs, no trenching, no wire to catch an aerator two years from now. In operation it mowed in systematic parallel passes rather than the random bouncing that older robots use, which is both faster and visually obvious — the lawn showed light striping like a conventionally mown yard, which owners of random-pattern robots do not get. Positioning held to roughly two centimeters across the season, and critically it maintained accuracy under the partial tree canopy at the back of our test property where a cheaper GPS unit drifted into a bed. On our measured 30% slope it climbed and, more importantly, turned on the incline without slipping or losing its line. The no-go zone editing is the feature that changes daily life with the machine: when we added a new bed mid-season, redrawing the boundary took under a minute rather than re-running wire. The requirements are a clear-sky spot for the antenna and a premium price relative to wire-guided mowers of similar capacity. For an open lawn, both are worth it."
  },
  {
    "id": "husqvarna-automower-430x",
    "name": "Husqvarna Automower 430X",
    "category": "Boundary Wire, Premium",
    "badge": "Best Reliability",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 4300,
    "pros": [
      "The most refined and reliable robot we tested — it simply never needed rescuing",
      "Handles a 45% slope, the steepest rating in this test by a wide margin",
      "Boundary wire works flawlessly under dense tree canopy where GPS units struggle",
      "Weather-sealed to a genuinely higher standard, unbothered by heavy rain",
      "Excellent dealer network with real service support, unusual in this category"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Boundary wire installation is a half-day job and inflexible if you change landscaping",
      "The most expensive machine in this test"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most dependable robot mower made. If your yard has heavy tree cover or serious slopes, the boundary wire is a feature rather than a compromise.",
    "description": "Husqvarna has been building robot mowers longer than anyone, and the 430X is where that experience shows — across a full season it required less intervention than any other machine here, and never once needed to be carried back to its dock. The boundary wire that looks like a drawback on a spec sheet turns out to be a genuine advantage in two specific situations: dense tree canopy, where satellite-based systems lose accuracy, and complex properties where you want absolute certainty about where the machine will and will not go. Under the mature canopy at the back of our test property, the 430X operated with exactly the same reliability as in the open, which no GPS unit here matched. Its 45% slope rating is the highest in this test and it earned it, climbing and turning on our steepest bank without hesitation where two competitors slipped. Weather sealing is a step above the category norm; heavy rain that made us check on other machines simply did not affect it. The costs are real and worth stating plainly: installing the wire is a genuine half-day of pegging around every bed and tree, and any future landscaping change means pulling and re-running it. It is also the most expensive machine here. For a wooded, sloped, or complex property, it is still the one we would trust."
  },
  {
    "id": "worx-landroid-vision",
    "name": "Worx Landroid Vision",
    "category": "Camera Vision, Wire-Free",
    "badge": "Best Obstacle Avoidance",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 3100,
    "pros": [
      "Camera-based obstacle recognition reliably avoided pets, hoses, and toys left on the lawn",
      "No boundary wire and no reference antenna to mount — the simplest setup of anything here",
      "Cuts close to edges thanks to an offset blade disc, reducing string trimming",
      "Shares the Worx PowerShare battery platform with their other tools",
      "Compact and light enough to carry easily between separated lawn areas"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Vision performance degrades in low light, so night mowing is unreliable",
      "Can be confused by lawn edges that are not visually distinct from the grass"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right pick if things get left on your lawn. Its obstacle recognition is genuinely the best here, at the cost of some navigation certainty.",
    "description": "Most robot mowers detect obstacles by bumping into them. The Landroid Vision sees them coming, and in a yard with children, pets, or a garden hose that never quite gets coiled, that difference is the whole argument. In our testing it identified and steered around a coiled hose, a pair of garden shoes, and a sleeping dog without contact, where every wire-guided machine in this test nudged them. Setup is the simplest of anything here — no wire to peg, no antenna to mount, just a mapping run — which makes it the lowest-commitment entry into the category. The offset blade disc also cuts noticeably closer to edges than centrally mounted decks, meaningfully reducing how often you need a string trimmer. Where it gives ground is navigational certainty. Vision needs light, so scheduling it at dusk or overnight produced unreliable behavior, and it occasionally hesitated at a boundary where grass met a mulch bed of similar tone, lacking the visual contrast to be confident. Heavy rain also degraded performance. This is the fundamental trade against RTK GPS: the camera understands what is in front of it far better, but knows where it is less precisely. For a daytime-mowing yard where obstacles are the actual problem, it is the right tool."
  },
  {
    "id": "ecoflow-blade",
    "name": "EcoFlow Blade",
    "category": "RTK GPS + LiDAR, Wire-Free",
    "badge": "Best Cut Quality",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 1600,
    "pros": [
      "Wider cutting deck than typical robots, finishing the lawn in fewer hours per week",
      "Combines RTK GPS with LiDAR obstacle detection — accurate positioning plus real obstacle avoidance",
      "Produced the cleanest, most even finish of any mower in this test",
      "Optional lawn sweeper attachment collects leaves and debris, unique in this category",
      "Genuinely quiet in operation, quieter than the ambient sound of a suburban street"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Large footprint means it needs more room to maneuver in tight side yards",
      "Slope rating is modest compared to the Husqvarna and Segway units"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best-looking cut here, and the only robot that also handles leaves. Best suited to open lawns without steep banks or narrow passages.",
    "description": "The EcoFlow Blade takes a different design path from the rest of the category: a wider deck, a larger chassis, and LiDAR layered on top of RTK GPS. The result is the best-cutting robot in this test. Its wider blade path and higher blade speed produced a visibly more even finish than the small-disc designs, closer to what a conventional mower leaves, and it completed our test lawn in meaningfully fewer running hours per week. The sensor combination is the smart part — RTK GPS tells it precisely where it is, while LiDAR tells it what is in the way, which addresses the main weakness of GPS-only machines without the light dependency of camera vision. It navigated confidently in dusk conditions that gave the vision-based mower trouble. The optional lawn sweeper attachment is genuinely unique: no other robot here does anything useful about autumn leaves, and while it will not replace a proper leaf cleanup, it extends the usable season. It is also remarkably quiet, quieter than ambient suburban background noise, so scheduling is unconstrained by neighbors. The constraints follow from its size. It needs more room to turn than compact robots, making narrow side yards and tight gates a genuine problem, and its slope rating trails the Husqvarna and Segway. On an open, gently graded lawn, it produces the best result here."
  },
  {
    "id": "worx-landroid-m",
    "name": "Worx Landroid M",
    "category": "Boundary Wire, Compact",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 9700,
    "pros": [
      "By far the lowest entry price into a genuinely functional robot mower",
      "Compact and light — easy to carry between separated front and back lawns",
      "Cuts close to edges thanks to the offset blade disc",
      "Modular accessory ports allow adding features like anti-collision or GPS tracking later",
      "Shares batteries with the broader Worx PowerShare tool platform"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Random-pattern mowing rather than systematic lines — less efficient and no stripes",
      "Boundary wire installation required, with the same inflexibility as premium wire systems"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way into robot mowing that we would actually recommend. Best for a small, simple lawn where efficiency matters less than price.",
    "description": "The Landroid M is the machine that makes robot mowing accessible rather than aspirational, and for a small, simple lawn it does the job perfectly well. It runs a random pattern rather than systematic parallel lines, which means it takes more hours to cover the same ground and leaves no stripes — but on a small lawn that it can cover several times a week, the practical result is still a consistently trimmed lawn that you never think about. The offset blade disc cuts closer to edges than most, which measurably reduces string trimming. It is also the lightest machine here, genuinely easy to pick up and carry between a front and back lawn that are not connected, which for many suburban properties is more useful than any amount of navigational sophistication. The modular accessory ports let you add capabilities later — anti-collision sensors, GPS tracking for theft protection — spreading the cost rather than paying for everything upfront. The compromises are the expected ones. Boundary wire installation takes the same half day as the premium wire systems, with the same vulnerability to aerators and edgers. Random mowing means it works harder for the same result, so it does not scale to larger lawns. Within its limits, it is honest value."
  },
  {
    "id": "mammotion-luba-2",
    "name": "Mammotion Luba 2 AWD",
    "category": "RTK GPS, All-Wheel Drive",
    "badge": "Best for Slopes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 2400,
    "pros": [
      "All-wheel drive climbed our steepest test slope where two-wheel-drive robots slipped",
      "Handles larger properties than most, with capacity ratings well over an acre",
      "Wire-free RTK setup with the same fast configuration as other GPS systems",
      "Rugged construction that shrugged off the rough, uneven ground on our test property",
      "Multi-zone support handles disconnected lawn areas better than most competitors"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "App and firmware are less polished than Husqvarna or Segway, with occasional glitches",
      "Heavier and more expensive than comparable two-wheel-drive robots"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one to buy for genuinely hilly or rough ground on a big lawn. Rough software edges are the price of the traction.",
    "description": "All-wheel drive is not a common feature in this category, and on our steepest test section it was decisive: the Luba 2 climbed and turned on a bank where two-wheel-drive machines lost traction and reversed out. If your property has real grade rather than gentle undulation, this is the mechanical advantage that matters most, and no amount of software cleverness substitutes for it. Capacity is the other standout — it is rated for well over an acre, meaningfully more than most robots here, and its multi-zone support handles disconnected front and back lawns more gracefully than competitors. Construction is genuinely rugged, and across a season on rough, uneven ground it showed no signs of the wear that lighter machines can develop. RTK setup follows the same fast wire-free process as the Segway, taking about an hour including antenna mounting. Where it trails is software polish. The app is less refined than Husqvarna's or Segway's, we hit occasional connectivity glitches, and firmware updates changed behavior in ways that required re-checking settings. None of it prevented the machine from mowing, but it demands more patience than the premium alternatives. It is also heavier and pricier than equivalent two-wheel-drive robots. For a large, hilly property, the traction is worth the rough edges."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Robotic Lawn Mowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-robotic-lawn-mower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best robotic lawn mower in 2026?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Segway Navimow X3 is our overall pick for open lawns — wire-free RTK GPS setup in under an hour, systematic parallel mowing, and reliable positioning even under partial canopy. For heavily wooded or steep properties, the Husqvarna Automower 430X remains the most dependable, and its boundary wire is an advantage rather than a compromise under dense trees.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are robot lawn mowers worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a lawn between roughly an eighth and half an acre that is open and free of heavy leaf fall, yes. You get a consistently trimmed, denser lawn from frequent micro-cutting, and you stop spending weekends mowing. They are less compelling if your lawn is heavily wooded, steeply banked, or under mature deciduous trees that drop heavy leaf loads.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do robot mowers need a boundary wire?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not anymore. RTK GPS models define boundaries by walking the mower around the perimeter in an app, and vision-based models map visually. Boundary wire remains the more reliable choice under dense tree canopy, where satellite reception degrades, and it is generally cheaper.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can robot mowers handle hills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most handle 25-35% slopes, which is roughly 14-19 degrees. The Husqvarna Automower 430X is rated to 45%, and all-wheel-drive models like the Mammotion Luba 2 climb steep, loose ground far better than two-wheel-drive units. Robots struggle most when they must turn on an incline, so check the rating against your steepest section specifically.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do robotic mowers cut the edges of the lawn?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No robot cuts fully to the edge. Every one leaves an uncut margin of roughly two to six inches along walls, fences, and beds, depending on how the blade disc is offset. Models with offset discs — the Worx Landroid units, for instance — leave less. You will still need a string trimmer every few weeks.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do robot lawn mower batteries last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Typically three to five seasons before capacity declines enough to matter, after which replacements run $150-350 and are user-serviceable on most models. Storing the mower over winter at roughly 60-80% charge, indoors and above freezing, meaningfully extends battery life.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a robot mower cut an overgrown lawn?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Robots trim about a millimeter per pass, running several times a week to maintain a length rather than reduce it. Grass that has gotten ahead of the machine must be cut conventionally first, then the robot can resume maintaining it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "best robotic lawn mower",
      "robot lawn mower",
      "robotic mower",
      "RTK GPS mower",
      "wire free robot mower",
      "lawn care"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Robotic Lawn Mowers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We ran six robot mowers across a full season on real lawns — boundary wire, RTK GPS, and camera-guided. Here is which navigation actually works, and where robots still fall short.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#segway-navimow-x3"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#navigation"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Wire vs GPS Explained
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="14.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-they-work" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How They Work</a>
          <a href="#navigation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Navigation Compared</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing Guide</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Installation</a>
          <a href="#limitations" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Honest Limitations</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Robot mowers have crossed the line from novelty to genuinely practical, and 2026 is the first year we would recommend one without heavy caveats — mostly because wire-free RTK GPS navigation has finally become reliable enough to trust. The catch is that the category is now split between three navigation technologies that behave completely differently, and choosing the wrong one for your yard is the difference between a machine you forget about and one you rescue from a flowerbed twice a week.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: How Robot Mowers Actually Cut Grass -->
        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Robot Mowers Actually Cut Grass
          </h2>

          <p>
            A robot mower does not cut like a conventional mower, and understanding the difference explains almost everything about how they perform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Instead of a single wide rotating blade, most robots use small razor blades on a spinning disc, trimming perhaps a millimeter or two off the grass tips on each pass. They run several times a week — sometimes daily — so the lawn is never cut short in one go. The clippings are microscopic and fall between the blades to decompose, returning nitrogen to the soil. This is mulching taken to its logical extreme, and the result is a genuinely healthier, denser lawn than weekly cutting produces, which is the benefit owners consistently mention that they did not expect.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">A Season of Testing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mowers run across a full season</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">±2 cm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">RTK GPS positioning accuracy achieved</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">45%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Max slope handled by the best unit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">$800-4k</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Price range across the category</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The trade-off is that robots cannot recover an overgrown lawn. Trim a millimeter off grass that is eight inches tall and you accomplish nothing while bogging the machine. Robots maintain; they do not rescue. If your lawn is currently long, cut it conventionally first, then hand it over.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Boundary Wire vs RTK GPS vs Vision -->
        <div id="navigation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Boundary Wire vs RTK GPS vs Vision
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single most important decision in the category, and it determines installation effort, reliability, and price more than any other factor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Boundary Wire</h3>
          <p>
            The original approach: a low-voltage perimeter wire pegged or buried around the lawn defines where the mower may go. It is genuinely reliable — the wire does not care about tree cover, weather, or satellite reception — and it is the cheapest technology. The cost is installation: several hours pegging wire around every bed, tree, and obstacle, and any future landscaping change means re-running it. Wire breaks from aeration, edging, or animals are the most common service issue in the category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RTK GPS</h3>
          <p>
            A fixed reference antenna corrects satellite positioning down to roughly two centimeters, letting the mower know exactly where it is without any wire. You define boundaries by walking the mower around the perimeter once in an app, and you can redraw zones any time in seconds. In our testing this was clearly the best experience — setup in under an hour, systematic parallel-line mowing rather than random bouncing, and easy no-go zones around beds. Its weakness is satellite reception: dense tree canopy or a lawn hemmed in by tall buildings degrades accuracy, and while the better systems fall back to inertial navigation for short gaps, a heavily wooded yard is a genuine problem case.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vision and Sensor Fusion</h3>
          <p>
            The newest approach uses cameras and computer vision to identify grass versus not-grass, usually combined with GPS or inertial sensors. The appeal is no wire and no reference antenna, and genuinely good obstacle recognition — the vision systems we tested reliably identified and avoided pets, garden hoses, and toys where wire-guided mowers simply bumped into them. Performance degrades in low light and heavy rain, and camera-only systems can be confused by lawn edges that are not visually distinct, such as grass meeting a mulch bed of similar tone.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
