            </table>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Roughly Double Your Actual Area</h3>
          <p>
            Obstacles, narrow passages, slopes, and irregular shapes all cost efficiency, and a mower operating at its rated limit runs constantly and still falls behind during spring growth. Sizing to roughly double your measured lawn area gives the machine margin to keep up when grass grows fastest, and lets it finish in fewer hours per week.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Slope Rating Against Your Steepest Section</h3>
          <p>
            Slope ratings are given as a percentage, not degrees — a 45% slope is about 24 degrees. Most robots handle 25-35%; a few reach 45%. Robots struggle most where a slope meets a boundary, since they must turn on the incline. If you have a steep bank, check the rating specifically and expect to trim that section by hand regardless.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Multiple Zones Need Multi-Zone Support</h3>
          <p>
            If your front and back lawns are separated by a driveway or gate, the mower cannot cross unless you create a passage. Wire systems handle this with a guide wire through a corridor; RTK systems can define separate zones, but most still need to be physically carried between them unless a mowable path connects them. Confirm how the model handles disconnected areas before buying.
