        <!-- SECTION 5: Installation and Setup: What to Expect -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installation and Setup: What to Expect
          </h2>

          <p>
            Setup effort varies enormously by technology, and it is the part homeowners most consistently underestimate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Boundary Wire Installation</h3>
          <p>
            Plan a half day. The wire runs around the entire mowable perimeter at a specified distance from hard edges — typically a hand's width from walls, closer to lawn edges the mower can overhang. You peg it into the turf every few feet and it disappears into the grass within a couple of weeks. Bury it a few inches deep if you aerate or edge regularly, because those are what cut wires. Keep a wire repair kit on hand from day one; you will eventually need it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RTK GPS Installation</h3>
          <p>
            Plan an hour. Mount the reference antenna where it has clear sky view — a roof edge, garage gable, or a supplied pole — and give it power. Then drive the mower around the perimeter from your phone to record the boundary, and drive around each bed or feature to define no-go zones. The whole map can be redrawn in minutes later, which is the genuine convenience advantage over wire.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Base Station Needs Power and Shelter</h3>
          <p>
            Every robot needs a charging dock on a level spot at the lawn edge with an outdoor outlet nearby. Ideally it sits in shade or under a cover, since sustained sun heat is hard on batteries and electronics. Plan this location before you buy — a lawn with no outdoor power anywhere near it becomes an electrician's visit you did not budget for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">First Two Weeks Are the Shakedown</h3>
          <p>
            Expect to make adjustments: a boundary that is slightly too close to a bed, a corner the mower cannot reach, a low branch that catches it, a downspout splash zone that leaves a soft rut. This settling-in period is normal for every system. After it, most owners report going weeks without touching the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: What Robot Mowers Still Cannot Do -->
        <div id="limitations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Robot Mowers Still Cannot Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            The category has improved enormously, and it is still worth being clear about where it falls short before spending $2,000.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>They do not edge or trim.</strong> Every robot leaves an uncut margin along walls, fences, and beds — typically two to six inches depending on how the deck is offset. You will still need a string trimmer every few weeks. Models with the blade disc offset toward one edge reduce this but do not eliminate it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>They cannot recover an overgrown lawn.</strong> Robots trim a millimeter at a time. If the grass gets ahead of the machine after a vacation or a wet spring week, you need to cut it conventionally before the robot can resume.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>They struggle with leaves.</strong> Autumn leaf fall defeats most robots — they either push leaves around or interpret the covered surface as an obstacle. Most owners store the mower once leaves start falling and clear leaves conventionally.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Theft is a real consideration.</strong> A visible $2,500 machine mowing an unfenced front lawn is a target. All models have PIN locks and most have GPS tracking that disables and locates a stolen unit, but check what your model includes and enable it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wildlife needs consideration.</strong> Robots running at night can injure hedgehogs, frogs, and other small animals. Schedule mowing during daylight hours, which is both safer for wildlife and lets you notice problems.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Blades Are Sharp and Kids Are Curious</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Robot mowers have lift and tilt sensors that stop the blades within fractions of a second, and their small blades sit well inside the body — they are far safer than conventional mowers. They are not toys, though. Teach children not to touch or lift a running mower, keep pets clear during operation, and use the app to pause mowing when the yard is in use.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintenance and Winter Storage -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintenance and Winter Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Robot mowers need less maintenance than any other mower type, but the small amount they do need is easy to forget precisely because the machine is invisible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Replace Blades Every 6-10 Weeks</h3>
          <p>
            The razor blades are small, inexpensive, and dull far faster than a conventional mower blade because they run daily. Dull blades tear rather than slice, producing brown tips and a lawn that looks subtly wrong for no obvious reason. A pack of blades costs a few dollars and takes five minutes to swap; this is the single highest-impact maintenance task.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean the Underside Monthly</h3>
          <p>
            Wet clippings build up around the blade disc and wheels, unbalancing the disc and adding drag. Unplug or power down first, tip the mower, and clear it with a brush or wooden scraper — most manufacturers advise against pressure washing because water intrudes into bearings and electronics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Charging Contacts Clean</h3>
          <p>
            Corroded or dirty dock contacts cause the most common owner-reported fault: a mower that returns to the dock but does not charge. A wipe with a cloth every few weeks prevents it entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Winter Storage</h3>
          <p>
            Charge the battery to roughly 60-80%, clean the machine thoroughly, and store it indoors above freezing. Storing a lithium battery fully depleted through a cold winter is the most reliable way to shorten its life. Leave the base station outside or bring it in per the manual, and check the boundary wire in spring before the first run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expect to Replace the Battery Eventually</h3>
          <p>
            Batteries typically last three to five seasons before capacity drops enough to matter. Replacements generally run $150-350 and are user-serviceable on most models. Factor this into the total cost when comparing against a conventional mower.
          </p>
        </div>

