        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best robotic lawn mower in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Segway Navimow X3 is our overall pick for open lawns — wire-free RTK GPS setup in under an hour, systematic parallel mowing, and reliable positioning even under partial canopy. For heavily wooded or steep properties, the Husqvarna Automower 430X remains the most dependable, and its boundary wire is an advantage rather than a compromise under dense trees.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are robot lawn mowers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a lawn between roughly an eighth and half an acre that is open and free of heavy leaf fall, yes. You get a consistently trimmed, denser lawn from frequent micro-cutting, and you stop spending weekends mowing. They are less compelling if your lawn is heavily wooded, steeply banked, or under mature deciduous trees that drop heavy leaf loads.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot mowers need a boundary wire?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not anymore. RTK GPS models define boundaries by walking the mower around the perimeter in an app, and vision-based models map visually. Boundary wire remains the more reliable choice under dense tree canopy, where satellite reception degrades, and it is generally cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can robot mowers handle hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most handle 25-35% slopes, which is roughly 14-19 degrees. The Husqvarna Automower 430X is rated to 45%, and all-wheel-drive models like the Mammotion Luba 2 climb steep, loose ground far better than two-wheel-drive units. Robots struggle most when they must turn on an incline, so check the rating against your steepest section specifically.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robotic mowers cut the edges of the lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No robot cuts fully to the edge. Every one leaves an uncut margin of roughly two to six inches along walls, fences, and beds, depending on how the blade disc is offset. Models with offset discs — the Worx Landroid units, for instance — leave less. You will still need a string trimmer every few weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do robot lawn mower batteries last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically three to five seasons before capacity declines enough to matter, after which replacements run $150-350 and are user-serviceable on most models. Storing the mower over winter at roughly 60-80% charge, indoors and above freezing, meaningfully extends battery life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a robot mower cut an overgrown lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Robots trim about a millimeter per pass, running several times a week to maintain a length rather than reduce it. Grass that has gotten ahead of the machine must be cut conventionally first, then the robot can resume maintaining it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying a Robot Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            The regrets in this category cluster around a few predictable errors, and all of them are avoidable before purchase.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying GPS for a Heavily Wooded Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                RTK GPS is the better technology on open ground and a genuine liability under mature canopy, where satellite reception degrades and the mower drifts or stalls. If your lawn is shaded by large trees, boundary wire remains the more reliable choice despite the installation work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to Your Exact Lawn Area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rated capacities assume open ground and near-daily running. Obstacles, slopes, and narrow passages all cost efficiency, and a mower at its rated limit falls behind during spring growth. Buy roughly double your measured area so the machine has margin when grass grows fastest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting About Autumn Leaves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most robots either push leaves around or read a leaf-covered lawn as an obstacle. Plan to store the mower once leaf fall starts and clear leaves conventionally, or choose a model with a sweeper attachment if extending the season matters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Neglecting Blade Changes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The small razor blades dull far faster than a conventional mower blade because they run nearly every day. Dull blades tear grass and leave brown tips, and owners blame the machine for a lawn that looks subtly wrong. Swap them every six to ten weeks — it takes five minutes and costs a few dollars.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Planning the Dock Location Before Buying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The charging station needs a level spot at the lawn edge with outdoor power nearby, ideally shaded. Discovering after purchase that the nearest outlet is on the wrong side of the house turns a weekend setup into an electrician's visit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
